<p>
The topic that wouldn&rsquo;t die. It started with a <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=14131#disqus_thread">quick post on Sacha&rsquo;s solution to a problem posed by David Pape</a>. I thought it was a nice solution but then <u>0-_</u>-0_ pointed out that <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=14133">abo-abo had already solved this problem with his tiny library</a>. So I wrote about that and prepared to move on.
</p>
<p>
I was not allowed to. Frequent commenter Phil remarked in my original post that <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=14131#comment-6942948974">he would use a keyboard macro</a>. He suggested that that would make sense for those of us who who seldom need to enter a sequence of ranges like Pape. In Pape&rsquo;s case this was something that he needed to do a lot so a solution like Sacha&rsquo;s or abo-abo&rsquo;s make sense. I agree with Phil. A keyboard macro is exactly how I would solve this problem given that I seldom have it.
</p>
<p>
This seems pretty obvious and might not be worth writing about except that Phil&rsquo;s solution showed me something that I didn&rsquo;t know: You can set the initial value of the counter with a <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>u</kbd> <kbd>n</kbd> where <i>n</i> is the desired initial value. That&rsquo;s a lot easier than using <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>x</kbd> <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>k</kbd> <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>c</kbd> to do the job.
</p>
<p>
As a final note, I think that Phil had a typo in his macro. As far as I can tell it should be
</p>
<pre class="example" id="org59e048e">
C-u 47 &lt;f3&gt; # &lt;f3&gt; , SPC &lt;f4&gt; &lt;f4&gt; &lt;f4&gt; &lt;f4&gt; &lt;f4&gt; &lt;f4&gt; &lt;f4&gt; &lt;f4&gt;
</pre>
<p>
At least that&rsquo;s what works for me.
</p>
<p>
It turns out that I regularly use a macro with a counter so I&rsquo;m really happy to learn that the counter can be set so easily.</p>
